vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4+x\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =14-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 14-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =14 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290490 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290490 #edb/ref 1069 "q-237018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290490 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290490 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $a=5i-4j+3k$ and $b=-7i+xj+k.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290490 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23ed6766-8e64-5c6e-b8a5-b8f25dcf697a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290490 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-7)+(-4)\\cdot x+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4x-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -4x-32 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-32 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290492 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290492 #edb/ref 1069 "q-237021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290492 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290492 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $c=⟨-3,\\sqrt{3},1⟩$ and $d=⟨x,0,-12⟩.$ Given that the sca